# A module's top-level `let NAME = <closure>` — a plain VALUE binding, not a
# `let NAME(params) = ...` function definition — called through a qualified
# `Module.NAME(args)` reference. Found investigating kexhq/kex#385's report
# of `Template.html(Kex.embed(...))` silently returning "" when the binding
# holding the compiled template lived in a non-entry module and was called
# through its qualified name.
#
# A module body has no separate "bare value" construct the way a file's own
# top level does (the synthetic-main collection that lets a bare `let x =
# do |y| ... end` at a FILE's top level bind straight to a variable) — so
# this registers as an ordinary zero-param FunctionDef, the only top-level-
# declaration machinery a module body has. Calling it with more args than
# its own declared zero used to fall straight through every real clause
# (correctly rejected, since none take an argument) to the dispatcher's
# generic "nothing matched" fallback — Optional.None — with no error at all,
# instead of evaluating the zero-arg binding for its value and applying the
# supplied args to THAT, exactly as the identical spelling at a file's own
# top level already did.
module Greet do
  let hello = do |name| "Hello, ${name}!" end
end

main do
  IO.printLine(Greet.hello("Ada"))
end
